Two words are used below with a specific meaning.

    NON-COMMERCIAL use is use by an individual, or by a group of individuals, that
    is not directed toward payment or commercial advantage: playing, learning,
    teaching, research, hobby modding, and building things you give away.

    COMMERCIAL use is anything else. In particular it includes use by or on behalf
    of a business or other organisation; use in producing, developing or supporting
    anything you sell, license, or offer for payment; use in a paid service, a paid
    course, or work you are paid to do; and use funded by advertising, sponsorship
    or subscription revenue that the use contributes to.

If your situation does not obviously fall on one side, it is a question and not a
verdict — see section 2.

1. NON-COMMERCIAL USE — granted to anyone.

   You may download, install, run and use the Software for any non-commercial
   purpose, with no fee and with no obligation to ask. Within that use, anything you
   author is entirely yours: this license places no condition on the mods, assets,
   data or worlds you make while using the Software, and no claim on them.

2. COMMERCIAL USE — not granted; ask.

   Commercial use requires written permission from the copyright holder. Ask, and
   say what you want to do. This is a door, not a wall: it exists so that the author
   knows who is building on this and on what terms, not to stop anyone working.

   Ask before you start, not after. Permission, once given, is given in writing and
   says what it covers.

3. SOURCE AND MODIFICATION — granted.

   You may read the source, keep copies of it, fork the repository, and modify the
   Software as much as you like for your own non-commercial use.

4. DISTRIBUTION OF A MODIFIED VERSION — granted when it adds something.

   You may distribute a modified version of the Software, publicly or privately, if
   all of the following hold:

   a. It adds substantial new functionality — capability the Software does not
      already provide, such that a person would choose your version for something it
      does and the Software does not. Rebuilding it, renaming it, bumping its
      version, re-hosting it, translating its documentation, changing its
      configuration, or making cosmetic or trivial edits is NOT adding
      functionality, and a version so changed is covered by section 5, not this one.

   b. It is clearly identified as a modified version and not as the Software itself,
      by a name that will not be mistaken for it.

   c. It carries this license, this copyright notice, and a visible link to the
      official repository named above.

   d. It is distributed non-commercially, and free of charge. A modified version is
      still this Software; sections 1 and 2 travel with it, and so do these terms
      for everyone who receives it.

   If you are unsure whether your change clears (a), ask. Permission is usually
   given, and asking costs less than guessing.

5. REDISTRIBUTION OF THE SOFTWARE ITSELF — not granted.

   You may not distribute the Software, or a build of it, to anyone else. In
   particular, and without limiting that sentence, you may not upload the mod jar or
   the MCP server to a mod-hosting site, a mirror, a file host, a package registry,
   an archive, a modpack, or any other place a person could obtain it from you
   instead of from the official repository — whether or not you charge for it, and
   whether or not you say where it came from.

   This is the thing this license is most concerned with. The Software is free for
   the people it is for, and always will be; it is simply distributed from one
   place, so that what a person downloads is what was actually released, at the
   version it claims to be, with the documentation that describes it.

   If you want people to have it, link them here.

6. BUNDLING WITH YOUR OWN WORK — ask first.

   If your project needs to ship the Software alongside it or inside it — a mod that
   embeds it, a modpack, a distribution, a container image, a course or a workshop
   handing it out — write and ask. This is not a refusal; it is a request to be told,
   so that the thing people receive can be a build that still exists and still works.

7. NAME.

   "MMCP" and "MCP Toolkit" name this project. Do not use either as the name of a
